                    correct: 1,
                    explanation: 'CRR is a blunt monetary tool: banks must deposit a fixed % of their net demand and time liabilities (essentially, deposits) with the RBI as a non-interest-bearing reserve. By increasing CRR, the RBI reduces the money available for banks to lend — contractionary. By reducing CRR, the RBI releases more funds into the banking system — expansionary. CRR changes are relatively rare; repo rate changes are the primary monetary policy tool.',
